'''Marsilac''' is one of 96 districts of [[São Paulo]], Brazil, located in the [[South Zone of São Paulo|extreme south]] tip of the city in the [[subprefecture of Parelheiros]].<ref name=dados>{{cite web|title=Dados Demográficos dos Distritos pertencentes as Subprefeituras|url=http://www.prefeitura.sp.gov.br/cidade/secretarias/subprefeituras/subprefeituras/dados_demograficos/index.php?p=12758|website=prefeitura.sp.gov.br|publisher=City of São Paulo|language=pt|access-date=28 December 2014|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150204055340/http://www.prefeitura.sp.gov.br/cidade/secretarias/subprefeituras/subprefeituras/dados_demograficos/index.php?p=12758|archive-date=4 February 2015}}</ref> Its name is a tribute to engineer [[Jose Alfredo Marsilac]], who developed many techniques for building roads and tunnels, even after losing 99% of his vision from being hit by a bomb in the [[Constitutionalist Revolution|Revolution of 1932]].

== History ==
Early settlement of the area owed mainly to the construction of the Mairinque–Santos by [[Estrada de Ferro Sorocabana]] connecting [[Mairinque]] and [[Santos, São Paulo|Santos]], completed in 1935. Along its extension, there were three stations in the district: Engineer Marsilac (which gave name to the surrounding neighborhood and later to its own district), Evangelista de Souza, and Rio de Campos.

== Features ==
Located near the [[Serra do Mar]] and almost entirely rural, Marsilac has the largest land area of the municipality, equivalent to all of the municipality's [[Central Zone of São Paulo|central area]]. It also has the lowest population density of all the districts, largely covered by reserves of the [[Atlantic Forest]].

This is the most remote district of the capital, located about 60–70 kilometers from ''[[Marco Zero (São Paulo)|Marco Zero]]'' and only about 10–15&nbsp;km from the Atlantic Ocean, which is visible from some points. It borders the municipalities of [[São Vicente, São Paulo|São Vicente]] and [[Itanhaém]]. A tiny area of the town lies at sea level, located in the [[Capivari River (Tietê River)|Capivari River]] valley.

It is the poorest district and has the lowest [[Human Development Index]] (HDI) of the city of São Paulo.

